{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 612 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2904056651ca070a03bb6cc2ce15f0ddb47f3dd9493fb589c56cdc473229505b" :edb/db-before-t 611 :edb/db-after-t 612 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533924 50 #inst "2026-10-05T13:19:21.024Z" 13194139533924 true] [17592186078306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266162 13194139533924 true] [17592186078306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266166 13194139533924 true] [17592186078307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266155 13194139533924 true] [17592186078307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266159 13194139533924 true] [17592186078741 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533924 true] [17592186078741 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes is equal to ${V}^{′}(4).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\sqrt{t}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{\\frac{1}{2}}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes is $\\frac{5}{4}$ gallons per minute." 13194139533924 true] [17592186266155 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266156 13194139533924 true] [17592186266155 1089 "q-341053" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266155 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533924 true] [17592186266155 1100 "The height $h$ (in feet) of a small rocket $t$ seconds after it is launched can be modeled by the function $h(t)=-16{t}^{2}+128t.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing with respect to time at the moment when its height is equal to $256$ feet.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the rocket's height when its height is $256$ feet is\n{{field-1}} feet per second." 13194139533924 true] [17592186266155 1113 #uuid "4c1266d9-11b2-531e-8076-104154430ad8" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266155 1145 "Since we are given that the rocket's height is $256$ feet, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(t) & =256 \\\\ -16{t}^{2}+128t & =256 \\\\ -16{t}^{2}+128t-256 & =0 \\\\ -16({t}^{2}-8t+16) & =0 \\\\ -16(t-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t-4 & =0 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing when $t=4$ seconds is equal to ${h}^{′}(4).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $h(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-16{t}^{2}+128t) \\\\  & =-32t+128,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=4$ gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(4)=-32(4)+128=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing when $t=4$ seconds is $0$ feet per second." 13194139533924 true] [17592186266156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266157 13194139533924 true] [17592186266156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266158 13194139533924 true] [17592186266156 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533924 true] [17592186266156 1041 "field-1" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266156 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266158 13194139533924 true] [17592186266156 1110 #uuid "6ea2b541-37cc-5099-9982-5472f6a7753c" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266157 1034 "128" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533924 true] [17592186266157 1111 #uuid "93aaae5b-304c-5cc1-a128-c608c839095a" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266158 1034 "0" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266158 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533924 true] [17592186266158 1111 #uuid "04a592ec-a5b2-5245-911f-4ade8d3308bf" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266159 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266160 13194139533924 true] [17592186266159 1089 "q-343424" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266159 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533924 true] [17592186266159 1100 "The concentration $C$ (in millimoles per liter) of a chemical in a reactor $t$ minutes after the reaction begins is modeled by the function $C(t)={t}^{3}+25$ for $0\\le t\\le 10.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the concentration is changing with respect to time at the moment when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is\n{{field-1}} millimoles per liter per minute." 13194139533924 true] [17592186266159 1113 #uuid "bcb5129b-3804-548c-a47a-d6b56b122986" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266159 1145 "Since we are given that the chemical's concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(t) & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3}+25 & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =125 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the chemical's concentration is changing when $t=5$ minutes is equal to ${C}^{′}(5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+25) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(5)=3(5)^{2}=75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the chemical's concentration when its concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is  $75$ millimoles per liter per minute." 13194139533924 true] [17592186266160 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266161 13194139533924 true] [17592186266160 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533924 true] [17592186266160 1041 "field-1" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266160 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266161 13194139533924 true] [17592186266160 1110 #uuid "181d8716-3c0a-5aff-9c35-c6ef0ef17dc0" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266161 1034 "75" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266161 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533924 true] [17592186266161 1111 #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266162 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266163 13194139533924 true] [17592186266162 1089 "q-343496" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266162 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533924 true] [17592186266162 1100 "The lateral surface area $A$ of a cone is increasing. The radius of the base of the cone is $r,$ and the slant height of the cone is always twice its radius. Find the rate of change of the lateral surface area of the cone with respect to its radius.\n\nThe rate of change of the lateral surface area of the cone with respect to its radius $r$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533924 true] [17592186266162 1113 #uuid "d10a00cb-697c-5a2f-9559-19cd6f8f6ef5" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266162 1145 "The area of the lateral surface area of the cone is given by\n\n$$\nA=A(r)=\\pi rℓ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base of the cone and $ℓ$ is its slant height.\n\nWe are given that $ℓ=2r,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the radius $r$ by substituting $ℓ=2r$ into the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi r(2r)=2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(r)$ with respect to its radius $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(2\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2r) \\\\  & =4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266164 13194139533924 true] [17592186266163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266165 13194139533924 true] [17592186266163 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533924 true] [17592186266163 1041 "field-1" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266163 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266164 13194139533924 true] [17592186266163 1110 #uuid "6fd21b00-71b4-51a2-a3d9-e416a5f840d2" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266164 1034 "4\\pi r" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533924 true] [17592186266164 1111 #uuid "38ba7e5a-239f-50a6-b2cd-33566d3c95f1" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266165 1034 "2\\pi r" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533924 true] [17592186266165 1111 #uuid "9e240240-0e38-5d6c-9044-c0aa67b65783" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266166 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266167 13194139533924 true] [17592186266166 1089 "q-343502" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266166 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533924 true] [17592186266166 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953428/assets/5fc1aff711355a147abac33a513434c55575c552ab58f0164ebc87222530f371.png)\n\nThe volume of a cube-shaped container is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is changing with respect to the side length at the moment when the face diagonal of the cube is equal to $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}$ centimeters is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266166 1113 #uuid "ce974f27-56cb-5d6d-a571-c1f4112d1075" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266166 1145 "Since we are given that the face diagonal of the cube is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$, its side length $s$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(s)={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{s}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=3(10)^{2}=300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is increasing when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$ is $300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266168 13194139533924 true] [17592186266167 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533924 true] [17592186266167 1041 "field-1" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266167 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266168 13194139533924 true] [17592186266167 1110 #uuid "7e113cfa-03c5-531e-9d48-c21ef10c1193" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266168 1034 "300" 13194139533924 true] [17592186266168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533924 true] [17592186266168 1111 #uuid